## Description

Resolves the gating legal unknown of this strategy: whether MinIO, licensed
AGPL-3.0, can be redistributed in the product's distribution model, and what
derivative-work posture applies when the Platform Operator bundles, deploys, and
manages it. The documented decision determines which upstream is productized
(MinIO, or the Apache-2.0 fallback named in the strategy) and unblocks all
shipping work, which the strategy explicitly holds back until clearance.

## Scope

- Obtain and document a legal determination on redistributing MinIO (AGPL-3.0)
  under the product's distribution model, including any conditions attached to
  clearance.
- Document the derivative-work posture for operator integration: whether
  bundling, deploying, and managing MinIO through the Platform Operator creates
  obligations beyond plain redistribution.
- Record a go/no-go per candidate store; on a no-go for MinIO, confirm (or reject
  with rationale) the Apache-2.0 fallback candidate (e.g. SeaweedFS) named in the
  strategy's open question.
- Communicate the outcome and the selected upstream to all downstream teams.

## Acceptance Criteria

- A documented legal determination exists stating whether MinIO (AGPL-3.0) can be
  redistributed under the product's distribution model, including any conditions
  attached.
- The derivative-work posture for operator integration is documented.
- The decision names the upstream to be productized (MinIO or the fallback), or
  records that no candidate cleared review.
- Downstream teams can proceed without further legal interpretation of the AGPL
  question.

## HLR Traceability

- HLR-1 (P0): legal review of redistributing the candidate object store (MinIO,
  AGPL-3.0) completed and documented, including the derivative-work posture for
  operator integration.
